WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.733
<v Tutor>Welcome to Part Two Daily.

2
00:00:01.733 --> 00:00:07.187
<v Tutor>It's Monday the sixteenth of November, and this is the primer for week six: cardiac intensive care.

3
00:00:07.187 --> 00:00:09.379
<v Tutor>Sixteen weeks to the written paper.

4
00:00:09.379 --> 00:00:13.814
<v Tutor>Cardiac is the block where the College shows you a picture and asks what it is.

5
00:00:13.814 --> 00:00:18.299
<v Tutor>The ECG has turned up more often than almost any other single item in the paper.

6
00:00:18.299 --> 00:00:25.537
<v Tutor>But the questions that sink candidates are the ones with an unstable patient attached to the rhythm, and that's where this week lives.

7
00:00:25.537 --> 00:00:27.219
<v Tutor>Here's the running order.

8
00:00:27.219 --> 00:00:29.564
<v Tutor>First, a short look back at last week.

9
00:00:29.564 --> 00:00:39.300
<v Tutor>Then the week's theme: the unstable arrhythmia, new atrial fibrillation in the critically ill, broad complex tachycardia, dynamic outflow obstruction and arrest after cardiac surgery.

10
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:45.773
<v Tutor>Then two landmark trials, an exam practice question from the 2021 second sitting, and a syllabus round-up of six items.

11
00:00:45.773 --> 00:00:46.640
<v Tutor>Let's go.

12
00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:49.037
<v Tutor>Last week.

13
00:00:49.037 --> 00:00:58.953
<v Tutor>Two answers on Wednesday the eleventh earned four and a half each, and they share a lesson: you knew the territory but left the specific, mark-bearing detail on the table.

14
00:00:58.953 --> 00:01:03.607
<v Tutor>The first was fluid responsiveness, 2020 second sitting, question seventeen.

15
00:01:03.607 --> 00:01:08.315
<v Tutor>The examiners wanted the limitations of pulse pressure variation spelled out.

16
00:01:08.315 --> 00:01:16.459
<v Tutor>Pulse pressure variation above about twelve percent predicts a response, with an area under the curve around 0.94 in Marik's meta-analysis.

17
00:01:16.459 --> 00:01:19.229
<v Tutor>But it only holds under strict conditions.

18
00:01:19.229 --> 00:01:23.383
<v Tutor>First, controlled ventilation with no spontaneous breathing effort.

19
00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:27.427
<v Tutor>Second, tidal volumes of at least eight millilitres per kilogram.

20
00:01:27.427 --> 00:01:28.978
<v Tutor>Third, sinus rhythm.

21
00:01:28.978 --> 00:01:30.696
<v Tutor>Fourth, a closed chest.

22
00:01:30.696 --> 00:01:35.294
<v Tutor>Fifth, no right ventricular failure and no raised intra-abdominal pressure.

23
00:01:35.294 --> 00:01:40.833
<v Tutor>In your answer those limitations drifted under passive leg raising, where they don't belong.

24
00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:43.437
<v Tutor>Hold on to the third one, sinus rhythm.

25
00:01:43.437 --> 00:01:51.359
<v Tutor>This week you'll meet a patient in fast atrial fibrillation, and the variation in pulse pressure from beat to beat will be meaningless.

26
00:01:51.359 --> 00:01:56.400
<v Tutor>Passive leg raising, by contrast, works in spontaneous breathing and in arrhythmia.

27
00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:07.246
<v Tutor>It returns roughly three hundred millilitres from the legs and splanchnic bed, but it must be judged on real-time cardiac output or aortic velocity time integral, not on blood pressure.

28
00:02:07.246 --> 00:02:12.827
<v Tutor>Monnet's meta-analysis found a pooled sensitivity and specificity of about 85 and 91 percent.

29
00:02:12.827 --> 00:02:18.962
<v Tutor>And it's unreliable with raised intra-abdominal pressure, and impractical with pelvic or leg fractures.

30
00:02:18.962 --> 00:02:25.593
<v Tutor>The second was keeping the continuous renal replacement circuit alive, 2014 first sitting, question twenty-nine.

31
00:02:25.593 --> 00:02:32.004
<v Tutor>The miss that matters: heparin-induced thrombocytopenia with thrombosis is a systemic prothrombotic disease.

32
00:02:32.004 --> 00:02:36.646
<v Tutor>Regional citrate keeps the filter running, but it doesn't treat the patient.

33
00:02:36.646 --> 00:02:51.180
<v Tutor>You must stop every source of heparin, flushes included, and start a systemic non-heparin anticoagulant: argatroban, cleared by the liver and falsely raising the INR; bivalirudin, cleared partly by the kidney; or danaparoid, monitored with anti-Xa levels.

34
00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:58.310
<v Tutor>An eighty-two-year-old on the ward is referred to you with an ECG, and nothing else.

35
00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:02.856
<v Tutor>That is the whole stem of question one in the 2026 first sitting.

36
00:03:02.856 --> 00:03:13.319
<v Tutor>Assess the patient and explain why you'd order each test, then discuss specific management in the first twenty-four hours if the arrhythmia makes them unstable.

37
00:03:13.319 --> 00:03:15.748
<v Tutor>The pass rate was 43.7 percent.

38
00:03:15.748 --> 00:03:21.852
<v Tutor>Fewer than half of candidates passed a question that every intensivist manages every week.

39
00:03:21.852 --> 00:03:25.215
<v Tutor>So let's set out how the College asks cardiac.

40
00:03:25.215 --> 00:03:33.373
<v Tutor>In the past question bank, Name That ECG Abnormality is tagged to seventeen questions, most recently in both 2026 sittings.

41
00:03:33.373 --> 00:03:39.041
<v Tutor>Cardiovascular physical examination appears eight times, all between 2009 and 2013.

42
00:03:39.041 --> 00:03:43.151
<v Tutor>Atrial fibrillation four times: 2012, 2021, 2024 and 2025.

43
00:03:43.151 --> 00:03:48.134
<v Tutor>Troubleshooting the temporary pacemaker, six times across its two forms.

44
00:03:48.134 --> 00:03:53.490
<v Tutor>Dynamic left ventricular outflow tract obstruction three times, twice in 2025.

45
00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:59.780
<v Tutor>And cardiac arrest after cardiac surgery twice, in 2022 and again in the 2026 second sitting.

46
00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:12.600
<v Tutor>The reading for this week is Oh's ninth edition: chapter thirty-one on cardiac arrhythmias, chapter thirty on resuscitation, chapter thirty-three on acute heart failure and chapter seventy-three on post-operative cardiac intensive care.

47
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:17.038
<v Tutor>Start with the framework that earns marks on any unstable rhythm.

48
00:04:17.038 --> 00:04:21.187
<v Tutor>Examiners reward a structure that a registrar could follow at 3 am.

49
00:04:21.187 --> 00:04:23.400
<v Tutor>First: is this patient unstable?

50
00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:29.595
<v Tutor>The resuscitation council's adverse features are shock, syncope, myocardial ischaemia and heart failure.

51
00:04:29.595 --> 00:04:33.855
<v Tutor>Any one of them changes the plan from drugs to electricity or pacing.

52
00:04:33.855 --> 00:04:35.846
<v Tutor>Second: classify the rhythm.

53
00:04:35.846 --> 00:04:37.008
<v Tutor>Fast or slow.

54
00:04:37.008 --> 00:04:38.336
<v Tutor>Narrow or broad.

55
00:04:38.336 --> 00:04:39.940
<v Tutor>Regular or irregular.

56
00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:43.757
<v Tutor>That gives you six boxes, and each box has its own algorithm.

57
00:04:43.757 --> 00:04:49.233
<v Tutor>Third: look for the cause, because in an eighty-two-year-old it is often outside the heart.

58
00:04:49.233 --> 00:04:55.871
<v Tutor>Drugs come first: beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, digoxin, and sometimes donepezil causing bradycardia.

59
00:04:55.871 --> 00:04:59.190
<v Tutor>Then electrolytes: potassium, magnesium and calcium.

60
00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:04.501
<v Tutor>Then ischaemia, then thyroid disease, then sepsis, then degenerative conduction disease.

61
00:05:04.501 --> 00:05:09.258
<v Tutor>Now the rationale for investigations, because that was half the 2026 question.

62
00:05:09.258 --> 00:05:12.743
<v Tutor>The twelve-lead ECG and rhythm strip define the rhythm.

63
00:05:12.743 --> 00:05:17.888
<v Tutor>Potassium and magnesium because both are arrhythmogenic and both are quickly fixable.

64
00:05:17.888 --> 00:05:21.428
<v Tutor>Troponin, to look for ischaemia as cause or consequence.

65
00:05:21.428 --> 00:05:24.360
<v Tutor>Thyroid function for new atrial fibrillation.

66
00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:29.911
<v Tutor>A digoxin level if they take it, with potassium, because toxicity is worse with hypokalaemia.

67
00:05:29.911 --> 00:05:33.900
<v Tutor>An arterial gas for acidosis and lactate as a measure of perfusion.

68
00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:37.623
<v Tutor>An echo for ventricular function and valves, and a chest film.

69
00:05:37.623 --> 00:05:39.910
<v Tutor>Every test needs a reason attached.

70
00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:43.420
<v Tutor>A list without reasons earns only a fraction of the marks.

71
00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:48.836
<v Tutor>Now treatment in the first twenty-four hours if the patient becomes unstable.

72
00:05:48.836 --> 00:05:53.911
<v Tutor>For an unstable tachyarrhythmia, synchronised DC cardioversion under sedation.

73
00:05:53.911 --> 00:06:05.772
<v Tutor>Current resuscitation guidance suggests a first biphasic shock of 120 to 150 joules for atrial fibrillation, escalating, and 70 to 120 joules for flutter and regular narrow complex tachycardia.

74
00:06:05.772 --> 00:06:14.505
<v Tutor>If three shocks fail, give amiodarone 300 milligrams over ten to twenty minutes and shock again, then 900 milligrams over twenty-four hours.

75
00:06:14.505 --> 00:06:20.642
<v Tutor>For an unstable bradycardia, atropine 500 micrograms, repeated to a maximum of three milligrams.

76
00:06:20.642 --> 00:06:26.425
<v Tutor>If that fails, isoprenaline or an adrenaline infusion at two to ten micrograms per minute.

77
00:06:26.425 --> 00:06:33.388
<v Tutor>Then transcutaneous pacing as a bridge, and transvenous pacing as the definitive bridge to a permanent device.

78
00:06:33.388 --> 00:06:40.765
<v Tutor>Atropine won't help a transplanted heart, and it rarely helps Mobitz two or complete heart block with a broad escape.

79
00:06:40.765 --> 00:06:45.013
<v Tutor>Then the specific treatments, which are where the top marks are.

80
00:06:45.013 --> 00:06:48.672
<v Tutor>Digoxin toxicity: digoxin-specific antibody fragments.

81
00:06:48.672 --> 00:06:55.340
<v Tutor>Hyperkalaemia: calcium first, ten millilitres of ten percent calcium chloride, then insulin and dextrose.

82
00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:59.798
<v Tutor>Beta-blocker overdose: high-dose insulin euglycaemia and glucagon.

83
00:06:59.798 --> 00:07:03.960
<v Tutor>Calcium channel blocker overdose: calcium and high-dose insulin.

84
00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:06.717
<v Tutor>Why do we synchronise the shock at all?

85
00:07:06.717 --> 00:07:09.776
<v Tutor>The answer is a story from Boston in the early 1960s.

86
00:07:09.776 --> 00:07:17.999
<v Tutor>In 1956 Paul Zoll showed that alternating current, straight from the mains through a transformer, could defibrillate a human heart through the closed chest.

87
00:07:17.999 --> 00:07:19.854
<v Tutor>It worked, but it was brutal.

88
00:07:19.854 --> 00:07:29.883
<v Tutor>Bernard Lown, a cardiologist at the Peter Bent Brigham Hospital, set out to use shocks deliberately to terminate arrhythmias that weren't arrests: atrial fibrillation, ventricular tachycardia.

89
00:07:29.883 --> 00:07:40.714
<v Tutor>Working with the engineer Baruch Berkovits from the American Optical Company, he found in animal experiments that alternating current burned muscle and could itself induce atrial and ventricular fibrillation.

90
00:07:40.714 --> 00:07:45.077
<v Tutor>They switched to direct current: a capacitor discharge with a defined waveform.

91
00:07:45.077 --> 00:07:46.230
<v Tutor>That was safer.

92
00:07:46.230 --> 00:07:48.236
<v Tutor>But Lown noticed something else.

93
00:07:48.236 --> 00:07:53.250
<v Tutor>There was a brief vulnerable window near the T wave, only a few hundredths of a second long.

94
00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:57.863
<v Tutor>A shock landing there could throw an organised rhythm into ventricular fibrillation.

95
00:07:57.863 --> 00:08:03.680
<v Tutor>So he timed the discharge to the R wave, well away from that window, and called the technique cardioversion.

96
00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:10.193
<v Tutor>The 1962 papers in JAMA described synchronised capacitor discharge, then its use for atrial fibrillation.

97
00:08:10.193 --> 00:08:18.936
<v Tutor>Lown went on to share the 1985 Nobel Peace Prize, accepting it for International Physicians for the Prevention of Nuclear War, a body he co-founded.

98
00:08:18.936 --> 00:08:20.617
<v Tutor>Here's the exam point.

99
00:08:20.617 --> 00:08:24.148
<v Tutor>Synchronisation exists to avoid shocking on the T wave.

100
00:08:24.148 --> 00:08:32.946
<v Tutor>That's why you synchronise for any organised rhythm with a pulse, and why you turn synchronisation off for ventricular fibrillation and pulseless VT.

101
00:08:32.946 --> 00:08:39.055
<v Tutor>In polymorphic VT the defibrillator can't reliably find an R wave, so you treat it as unsynchronised.

102
00:08:39.055 --> 00:08:45.500
<v Tutor>And after each synchronised shock, check that the sync mode hasn't reset, because on many machines it does.

103
00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:47.637
<v Tutor>Here's a pause and answer.

104
00:08:47.637 --> 00:08:55.504
<v Tutor>Your patient in fast atrial fibrillation with a blood pressure of 75 over 40 has had a synchronised shock, and is now in ventricular fibrillation.

105
00:08:55.504 --> 00:08:59.132
<v Tutor>Name the most likely technical cause, and say what you do next.

106
00:08:59.132 --> 00:09:00.460
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

107
00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:14.657
<v Tutor>The most likely cause is that the shock wasn't synchronised, or the device marked a T wave or artefact as the R wave, so the discharge fell in the vulnerable period.

108
00:09:14.657 --> 00:09:16.843
<v Tutor>Next: this is now a cardiac arrest.

109
00:09:16.843 --> 00:09:23.860
<v Tutor>Check that sync is off, defibrillate immediately unsynchronised at maximum energy, and follow the advanced life support algorithm.

110
00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:27.983
<v Tutor>Now new-onset atrial fibrillation in the critically ill.

111
00:09:27.983 --> 00:09:36.127
<v Tutor>This is the most examined rhythm problem in the paper, and the College wants the intensivist's version, not the cardiology clinic's.

112
00:09:36.127 --> 00:09:37.290
<v Tutor>It's common.

113
00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:46.656
<v Tutor>The 2024 European guideline notes sepsis is the most common precipitating factor, with atrial fibrillation in roughly 9 to 20 percent of septic patients.

114
00:09:46.656 --> 00:09:48.983
<v Tutor>It also marks a worse prognosis.

115
00:09:48.983 --> 00:09:59.453
<v Tutor>Most of the time it's driven by the acute illness: endogenous and infused catecholamines, atrial stretch from fluid, inflammation, electrolyte loss and lines in the atrium.

116
00:09:59.453 --> 00:10:08.237
<v Tutor>The governing guideline is the 2024 European Society of Cardiology guideline for atrial fibrillation, written with the cardiothoracic surgeons.

117
00:10:08.237 --> 00:10:10.564
<v Tutor>Its framework is called AF-CARE.

118
00:10:10.564 --> 00:10:13.007
<v Tutor>C, comorbidities and risk factors.

119
00:10:13.007 --> 00:10:15.567
<v Tutor>A, avoid stroke and thromboembolism.

120
00:10:15.567 --> 00:10:18.708
<v Tutor>R, reduce symptoms by rate and rhythm control.

121
00:10:18.708 --> 00:10:21.442
<v Tutor>E, evaluation and dynamic reassessment.

122
00:10:21.442 --> 00:10:24.467
<v Tutor>Note the order: the precipitant comes first.

123
00:10:24.467 --> 00:10:32.669
<v Tutor>In the unit, that means treating sepsis, hypoxia, hypovolaemia and potassium and magnesium, and cutting catecholamines where you can.

124
00:10:32.669 --> 00:10:35.985
<v Tutor>Three changes from earlier guidance are quotable.

125
00:10:35.985 --> 00:10:40.580
<v Tutor>First, the stroke score is now CHA2DS2-VA: female sex has been removed.

126
00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:47.153
<v Tutor>A score of two or more means anticoagulation is recommended, and a score of one means it should be considered.

127
00:10:47.153 --> 00:10:52.353
<v Tutor>Bleeding scores should not be used to decide on starting or withdrawing anticoagulation.

128
00:10:52.353 --> 00:10:57.554
<v Tutor>Second, the early cardioversion window has shrunk from forty-eight hours to twenty-four.

129
00:10:57.554 --> 00:11:05.463
<v Tutor>Beyond twenty-four hours, cardioversion needs three weeks of therapeutic anticoagulation first, or a transoesophageal echo excluding clot.

130
00:11:05.463 --> 00:11:12.560
<v Tutor>Third, wait-and-see is acceptable in a stable patient, because many paroxysms revert by themselves once the driver settles.

131
00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:15.870
<v Tutor>In the unit, rate control usually comes first.

132
00:11:15.870 --> 00:11:17.864
<v Tutor>A beta-blocker is first line.

133
00:11:17.864 --> 00:11:23.415
<v Tutor>Metoprolol, or esmolol if you want something titratable with a half-life of about nine minutes.

134
00:11:23.415 --> 00:11:28.428
<v Tutor>Diltiazem or verapamil are options only if the ejection fraction is above 40 percent.

135
00:11:28.428 --> 00:11:32.308
<v Tutor>Digoxin is slow, but useful with poor left ventricular function.

136
00:11:32.308 --> 00:11:35.919
<v Tutor>Magnesium is cheap and helps with both rate and conversion.

137
00:11:35.919 --> 00:11:42.278
<v Tutor>Amiodarone does both rate and rhythm and is the default when the blood pressure won't tolerate a beta-blocker.

138
00:11:42.278 --> 00:11:46.751
<v Tutor>Amiodarone is the anti-arrhythmic you'll be asked about, so know its harms.

139
00:11:46.751 --> 00:11:52.626
<v Tutor>Intravenously, it causes hypotension, largely from its solvent, plus bradycardia and QT prolongation.

140
00:11:52.626 --> 00:11:55.051
<v Tutor>It's an irritant to peripheral veins.

141
00:11:55.051 --> 00:12:04.375
<v Tutor>With longer use come thyroid dysfunction in both directions, pulmonary toxicity, hepatitis and drug interactions: it raises digoxin levels and the INR with warfarin.

142
00:12:04.375 --> 00:12:05.345
<v Tutor>Two traps.

143
00:12:05.345 --> 00:12:12.890
<v Tutor>Never give AV nodal blockers to pre-excited atrial fibrillation, the irregular, broad, bizarre tachycardia of Wolff-Parkinson-White.

144
00:12:12.890 --> 00:12:18.926
<v Tutor>Blocking the node pushes conduction down the accessory pathway and can trigger ventricular fibrillation.

145
00:12:18.926 --> 00:12:20.920
<v Tutor>Use procainamide, or a shock.

146
00:12:21.100 --> 00:12:26.788
<v Tutor>And in the ICU, cardioversion often fails or the rhythm relapses while the driver persists.

147
00:12:26.788 --> 00:12:29.660
<v Tutor>Say so in your answer: it shows judgement.

148
00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:31.503
<v Tutor>Pause and answer.

149
00:12:31.503 --> 00:12:39.062
<v Tutor>A septic patient on noradrenaline develops atrial fibrillation at 150 beats per minute with a blood pressure of 95 over 55.

150
00:12:39.062 --> 00:12:41.312
<v Tutor>The AF started eight hours ago.

151
00:12:41.312 --> 00:12:43.967
<v Tutor>Their ejection fraction is 35 percent.

152
00:12:43.967 --> 00:12:46.160
<v Tutor>Give your first three actions.

153
00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:47.660
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

154
00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:55.027
<v Tutor>First: correct the drivers.

155
00:12:55.027 --> 00:13:01.939
<v Tutor>Potassium to around 4.5, magnesium replaced, oxygenation and volume status checked, and the noradrenaline reviewed.

156
00:13:01.939 --> 00:13:06.435
<v Tutor>Second: the patient isn't in shock from the rhythm, so treat with drugs.

157
00:13:06.435 --> 00:13:10.875
<v Tutor>With an ejection fraction of 35 percent, avoid diltiazem and verapamil.

158
00:13:10.875 --> 00:13:16.157
<v Tutor>Amiodarone is reasonable, with cautious beta-blockade as the alternative if tolerated.

159
00:13:16.157 --> 00:13:24.868
<v Tutor>Third: because the onset is clearly under twenty-four hours, cardioversion without prior anticoagulation is acceptable if the patient deteriorates.

160
00:13:24.868 --> 00:13:31.780
<v Tutor>Then calculate CHA2DS2-VA, and decide about anticoagulation once the bleeding risk from the acute illness is clear.

161
00:13:32.180 --> 00:13:37.851
<v Tutor>Next, the broad complex tachycardia, which is where ECG questions go to hurt you.

162
00:13:37.851 --> 00:13:48.111
<v Tutor>2021 second sitting, question twenty-three, gave a broad complex tachycardia at about 200 per minute with a QRS of 150 milliseconds and a northwest axis.

163
00:13:48.111 --> 00:13:53.336
<v Tutor>It then asked for the differential and how to separate the two on the ECG.

164
00:13:53.336 --> 00:14:02.831
<v Tutor>The safe rule first: a broad complex tachycardia is ventricular tachycardia until proven otherwise, especially with structural heart disease.

165
00:14:02.831 --> 00:14:07.164
<v Tutor>Verapamil given to VT on the assumption that it's SVT kills.

166
00:14:07.164 --> 00:14:10.414
<v Tutor>To discriminate, use the Brugada algorithm.

167
00:14:10.414 --> 00:14:12.581
<v Tutor>Any one of these means VT.

168
00:14:12.581 --> 00:14:15.767
<v Tutor>One, no RS complex in any precordial lead.

169
00:14:15.767 --> 00:14:20.674
<v Tutor>Two, an R to S interval over 100 milliseconds in any precordial lead.

170
00:14:20.674 --> 00:14:25.836
<v Tutor>Three, atrioventricular dissociation, including capture and fusion beats.

171
00:14:25.836 --> 00:14:29.404
<v Tutor>Four, VT morphology criteria in V1 to V2 and V6.

172
00:14:29.404 --> 00:14:33.482
<v Tutor>Also mention extreme axis deviation, the northwest axis.

173
00:14:33.482 --> 00:14:35.458
<v Tutor>It strongly favours VT.

174
00:14:35.458 --> 00:14:38.070
<v Tutor>Polymorphic VT is the other face.

175
00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:42.340
<v Tutor>If the QT is long before the run, it's torsades de pointes.

176
00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:55.594
<v Tutor>Treatment is two grams of magnesium sulphate intravenously, potassium kept high-normal, every QT-prolonging drug stopped, and the heart rate raised with isoprenaline or overdrive pacing, because torsades is pause-dependent.

177
00:14:55.594 --> 00:15:00.292
<v Tutor>If the QT is normal, think ischaemia first, and get the coronary angiogram.

178
00:15:00.292 --> 00:15:08.838
<v Tutor>And the young man after an out-of-hospital arrest, with coved ST elevation sloping into inverted T waves in V1 and V2, has the Brugada pattern.

179
00:15:08.838 --> 00:15:12.970
<v Tutor>Know that fever unmasks it and sodium channel blockers worsen it.

180
00:15:12.970 --> 00:15:17.668
<v Tutor>For an electrical storm, isoprenaline and quinidine are the drugs you name.

181
00:15:17.668 --> 00:15:24.120
<v Tutor>The governing document is the 2022 European guideline on ventricular arrhythmias and sudden cardiac death.

182
00:15:24.520 --> 00:15:28.117
<v Tutor>Now dynamic left ventricular outflow tract obstruction.

183
00:15:28.117 --> 00:15:33.598
<v Tutor>You scored seven on this on the twelfth of October, 2025 first sitting, question twelve.

184
00:15:33.598 --> 00:15:42.961
<v Tutor>The missing points were the mitral regurgitation that comes from systolic anterior motion, and echo as the test that makes the diagnosis and guides therapy.

185
00:15:42.961 --> 00:15:51.011
<v Tutor>The 2019 first sitting, question eight, asked for predisposing and precipitating factors, the signs, and the principles of treatment.

186
00:15:51.011 --> 00:16:06.312
<v Tutor>The examiners' comment is worth remembering word for word: "Candidates who did poorly did not know how to manage outflow tract obstruction and recommended dangerous therapies, including inotropic therapy and vasodilator agents." The pass rate was 65.9 percent.

187
00:16:06.312 --> 00:16:08.710
<v Tutor>The mechanism explains everything.

188
00:16:08.710 --> 00:16:16.474
<v Tutor>A small, hyperdynamic, hypertrophied ventricle ejects so fast that the anterior mitral leaflet is pulled into the outflow tract.

189
00:16:16.474 --> 00:16:20.756
<v Tutor>Anything that empties the ventricle or speeds it up makes it worse.

190
00:16:20.756 --> 00:16:29.948
<v Tutor>So: predisposing, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, hypertensive hypertrophy, and the ventricle just relieved of aortic stenosis by valve replacement or TAVI.

191
00:16:29.948 --> 00:16:36.000
<v Tutor>Precipitating: hypovolaemia, vasodilatation from sepsis or anaesthesia, tachycardia and inotropes.

192
00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:38.178
<v Tutor>Treatment reverses each one.

193
00:16:38.178 --> 00:16:39.288
<v Tutor>Give volume.

194
00:16:39.288 --> 00:16:43.283
<v Tutor>Use a pure vasoconstrictor such as phenylephrine or vasopressin.

195
00:16:43.283 --> 00:16:46.502
<v Tutor>Slow the heart with a beta-blocker, often esmolol.

196
00:16:46.502 --> 00:16:47.945
<v Tutor>Stop the inotrope.

197
00:16:47.945 --> 00:16:49.387
<v Tutor>Keep sinus rhythm.

198
00:16:49.387 --> 00:16:58.100
<v Tutor>And diagnose it on echo: systolic anterior motion, a late-peaking dagger-shaped outflow Doppler, and a gradient of 30 millimetres of mercury or more.

199
00:16:58.500 --> 00:16:59.845
<v Tutor>Pause and answer.

200
00:16:59.845 --> 00:17:03.449
<v Tutor>Day one after aortic valve replacement for severe stenosis.

201
00:17:03.449 --> 00:17:10.818
<v Tutor>The patient is hypotensive, tachycardic at 120, and the nurse has just started dobutamine, after which the pressure fell further.

202
00:17:10.818 --> 00:17:14.260
<v Tutor>A new systolic murmur is heard at the left sternal edge.

203
00:17:14.260 --> 00:17:17.541
<v Tutor>What's happening, and what are your next three moves?

204
00:17:17.541 --> 00:17:18.940
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

205
00:17:24.340 --> 00:17:31.620
<v Tutor>Dynamic outflow obstruction in a hypertrophied ventricle freshly relieved of its stenosis, made worse by the inotrope.

206
00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:33.238
<v Tutor>Stop the dobutamine.

207
00:17:33.238 --> 00:17:38.438
<v Tutor>Give a fluid bolus and start a pure vasoconstrictor, phenylephrine or vasopressin.

208
00:17:38.438 --> 00:17:48.260
<v Tutor>Then slow the rate, with esmolol if the pressure allows, and confirm on echo: systolic anterior motion, mitral regurgitation and a dagger-shaped outflow gradient.

209
00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:54.358
<v Tutor>Pacing deserves a minute, because troubleshooting the temporary pacemaker has been asked six times.

210
00:17:54.358 --> 00:17:58.566
<v Tutor>Know the pacing threshold: the smallest current that reliably captures.

211
00:17:58.566 --> 00:18:06.501
<v Tutor>Pace about ten beats above the intrinsic rate, turn the output down until capture is lost, then set the output at about twice that threshold.

212
00:18:06.501 --> 00:18:08.631
<v Tutor>Sensitivity is the reverse idea.

213
00:18:08.631 --> 00:18:12.305
<v Tutor>It decides how small an intrinsic signal the box will notice.

214
00:18:12.305 --> 00:18:17.897
<v Tutor>You set it so the pacemaker doesn't compete with the patient's own rhythm and fire on the T wave.

215
00:18:17.897 --> 00:18:23.809
<v Tutor>When the nurse tells you the output has crept up all shift to keep capture, list the reversible causes.

216
00:18:23.809 --> 00:18:28.122
<v Tutor>The wire has moved, or there's oedema and fibrosis at the epicardial tip.

217
00:18:28.122 --> 00:18:32.170
<v Tutor>Electrolytes and acid-base: potassium too high or too low, acidosis.

218
00:18:32.170 --> 00:18:34.460
<v Tutor>And drugs that raise the threshold.

219
00:18:34.460 --> 00:18:48.200
<v Tutor>Then the fixes from the 2013 first-sitting answer: increase output, check every connection, reverse the polarity, swap the box and cables, convert to unipolar pacing with a skin electrode, and use transcutaneous or transvenous pacing as the fallback.

220
00:18:48.200 --> 00:18:52.460
<v Tutor>The second trace in that question showed pacemaker-mediated tachycardia.

221
00:18:52.640 --> 00:19:00.221
<v Tutor>That's a re-entry loop: a ventricular beat conducts back to the atria, the atrial lead senses it and triggers another paced beat.

222
00:19:00.221 --> 00:19:05.920
<v Tutor>You break it by lengthening the post-ventricular atrial refractory period or changing the mode.

223
00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:11.708
<v Tutor>Your own weak spot this block is the haemodynamically unstable cardiac surgical patient.

224
00:19:11.708 --> 00:19:16.591
<v Tutor>You earned four and a half on the 2016 first sitting, question six, a week ago.

225
00:19:16.591 --> 00:19:20.240
<v Tutor>That error is worth naming again because it'll come back.

226
00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:28.715
<v Tutor>A low wedge pressure and a low central venous pressure with a low cardiac index mean the ventricle is underfilled, so the first move is volume.

227
00:19:28.715 --> 00:19:34.720
<v Tutor>A high systemic vascular resistance, 1,700 in that question, means the circulation is clamped down.

228
00:19:34.720 --> 00:19:39.098
<v Tutor>It calls for afterload reduction and inotropy, not more noradrenaline.

229
00:19:39.098 --> 00:19:48.640
<v Tutor>And whenever a post-operative cardiac patient deteriorates with rising filling pressures and falling output, consider tamponade first: echo, and call the surgeon.

230
00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:54.363
<v Tutor>Which brings us to arrest after cardiac surgery, examined in 2022 and again in 2026.

231
00:19:54.363 --> 00:20:02.579
<v Tutor>The governing document is the Society of Thoracic Surgeons expert consensus, Dunning and colleagues, Annals of Thoracic Surgery, 2017.

232
00:20:02.579 --> 00:20:07.092
<v Tutor>It departs from standard advanced life support in ways examiners love.

233
00:20:07.092 --> 00:20:20.283
<v Tutor>For ventricular fibrillation or pulseless VT, give up to three sequential shocks before starting compressions, because a shock within the first minute works and external compressions risk tearing grafts and suture lines.

234
00:20:20.283 --> 00:20:24.044
<v Tutor>Amiodarone 300 milligrams follows the third failed shock.

235
00:20:24.044 --> 00:20:32.375
<v Tutor>For asystole or severe bradycardia with epicardial wires in place, connect them and pace at maximum output, DDD at 80 to 100 per minute.

236
00:20:32.375 --> 00:20:34.458
<v Tutor>Atropine is not recommended.

237
00:20:34.458 --> 00:20:42.211
<v Tutor>If none of that works, resternotomy within five minutes, and it stays part of the protocol until the tenth post-operative day.

238
00:20:42.211 --> 00:20:45.509
<v Tutor>Internal massage is more effective than external.

239
00:20:45.509 --> 00:20:47.939
<v Tutor>Adrenaline is not given routinely.

240
00:20:47.939 --> 00:20:59.800
<v Tutor>The consensus says to give it only if an experienced clinician directs, because a full one milligram dose on a heart that restarts can cause severe hypertension and bleeding from fresh anastomoses.

241
00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:04.723
<v Tutor>If the monitor shows a paced rhythm with no output, briefly pause the pacing.

242
00:21:04.723 --> 00:21:06.620
<v Tutor>It can mask underlying VF.

243
00:21:07.020 --> 00:21:08.351
<v Tutor>Pause and answer.

244
00:21:08.351 --> 00:21:14.526
<v Tutor>Ten minutes after arriving from theatre, a patient after coronary bypass goes into ventricular fibrillation.

245
00:21:14.526 --> 00:21:16.868
<v Tutor>Their epicardial wires are attached.

246
00:21:16.868 --> 00:21:19.796
<v Tutor>What's your sequence for the first two minutes?

247
00:21:19.796 --> 00:21:21.180
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

248
00:21:26.580 --> 00:21:31.362
<v Tutor>Confirm the rhythm and call for help, including the surgeon and the resternotomy kit.

249
00:21:31.362 --> 00:21:35.887
<v Tutor>Give up to three sequential shocks within the first minute, before compressions.

250
00:21:35.887 --> 00:21:43.599
<v Tutor>If VF persists after the third, start external compressions, give amiodarone 300 milligrams, and prepare for resternotomy within five minutes.

251
00:21:43.599 --> 00:21:45.450
<v Tutor>Withhold routine adrenaline.

252
00:21:45.450 --> 00:21:52.340
<v Tutor>Keep ventilation at 100 percent oxygen and check the airway and ventilator, and turn off the pacing if it might be masking VF.

253
00:21:52.740 --> 00:21:55.412
<v Tutor>Three numbers to remember this week.

254
00:21:55.412 --> 00:22:03.305
<v Tutor>Twenty-four hours: the new cut-off for cardioversion without prior anticoagulation or TOE, in the 2024 European guideline.

255
00:22:03.305 --> 00:22:11.199
<v Tutor>Three shocks: the stacked sequence for VF after cardiac surgery, before compressions and before amiodarone 300 milligrams.

256
00:22:11.199 --> 00:22:13.871
<v Tutor>Two grams of magnesium for torsades.

257
00:22:13.871 --> 00:22:19.700
<v Tutor>And with it, the reminder that a broad complex tachycardia is VT until proven otherwise.

258
00:22:21.100 --> 00:22:26.624
<v Tutor>Two trials this week, both about choices we make every day in cardiac intensive care.

259
00:22:26.624 --> 00:22:34.939
<v Tutor>First, the Cardiothoracic Surgical Trials Network trial of rate versus rhythm control for atrial fibrillation after cardiac surgery.

260
00:22:34.939 --> 00:22:39.156
<v Tutor>Gillinov and colleagues, New England Journal of Medicine, 2016.

261
00:22:39.156 --> 00:22:42.958
<v Tutor>It's chapter fifty of Landmark Studies in Critical Care.

262
00:22:42.958 --> 00:22:49.491
<v Tutor>The question: post-operative atrial fibrillation affects roughly a third of cardiac surgical patients.

263
00:22:49.491 --> 00:22:55.253
<v Tutor>Should we chase sinus rhythm with amiodarone and cardioversion, or just control the rate?

264
00:22:55.253 --> 00:23:02.380
<v Tutor>Population: patients having elective coronary or valve surgery who developed post-operative atrial fibrillation.

265
00:23:02.380 --> 00:23:04.043
<v Tutor>523 were randomised.

266
00:23:04.043 --> 00:23:10.339
<v Tutor>Intervention: rhythm control with amiodarone, plus cardioversion if atrial fibrillation persisted.

267
00:23:10.339 --> 00:23:13.725
<v Tutor>Comparator: rate control with rate-slowing drugs.

268
00:23:13.725 --> 00:23:17.645
<v Tutor>Primary outcome: total days in hospital within sixty days.

269
00:23:17.645 --> 00:23:21.565
<v Tutor>5.1 days with rate control versus 5.0 with rhythm control.

270
00:23:21.565 --> 00:23:22.872
<v Tutor>No difference.

271
00:23:22.872 --> 00:23:33.860
<v Tutor>Secondary results: by sixty days, freedom from atrial fibrillation was 93.8 percent with rate control and 97.9 percent with rhythm control, a difference that wasn't significant.

272
00:23:34.040 --> 00:23:38.768
<v Tutor>About a quarter of patients in each arm didn't stay on their assigned strategy.

273
00:23:38.768 --> 00:23:42.464
<v Tutor>Rhythm control brought the expected amiodarone side effects.

274
00:23:42.464 --> 00:23:50.073
<v Tutor>Main criticisms: crossover blurred the comparison, it was open label, and the sixty-day horizon says nothing about long-term stroke.

275
00:23:50.073 --> 00:23:54.421
<v Tutor>What it changed: it gave intensivists permission to use either strategy.

276
00:23:54.421 --> 00:24:05.562
<v Tutor>Most post-operative atrial fibrillation is self-limiting, so rate control is a legitimate first choice in the stable patient, and you reserve rhythm control for symptoms or haemodynamic compromise.

277
00:24:05.562 --> 00:24:18.280
<v Tutor>How to quote it in an answer: "In the CTSN trial, rate and rhythm control for post-operative atrial fibrillation gave the same hospital stay, 5.1 versus 5.0 days, with over 90 percent in sinus rhythm at sixty days either way."

278
00:24:18.680 --> 00:24:20.628
<v Tutor>Second, CULPRIT-SHOCK.

279
00:24:20.628 --> 00:24:25.109
<v Tutor>Thiele and colleagues, New England Journal of Medicine, 2017.

280
00:24:25.109 --> 00:24:28.096
<v Tutor>Chapter fifty-one of Landmark Studies.

281
00:24:28.096 --> 00:24:35.758
<v Tutor>The question: in cardiogenic shock complicating myocardial infarction, most patients have multivessel disease.

282
00:24:35.758 --> 00:24:42.446
<v Tutor>Should the interventionist open every critical lesion while they're there, or only the culprit?

283
00:24:42.446 --> 00:24:50.108
<v Tutor>Population: 706 patients with acute myocardial infarction, cardiogenic shock and multivessel coronary disease.

284
00:24:50.108 --> 00:24:56.147
<v Tutor>Intervention: culprit-lesion-only PCI, with staged revascularisation later if needed.

285
00:24:56.147 --> 00:25:02.251
<v Tutor>Comparator: immediate multivessel PCI, including attempts on chronic total occlusions.

286
00:25:02.251 --> 00:25:09.004
<v Tutor>Primary outcome: death or severe renal failure needing renal replacement therapy at thirty days.

287
00:25:09.004 --> 00:25:13.420
<v Tutor>45.9 versus 55.4 percent, relative risk 0.83, p equals 0.01.

288
00:25:13.420 --> 00:25:16.991
<v Tutor>The driver was death: 43.3 versus 51.6 percent.

289
00:25:16.991 --> 00:25:22.770
<v Tutor>Renal replacement therapy was 11.6 versus 16.4 percent, which wasn't significant.

290
00:25:22.770 --> 00:25:38.160
<v Tutor>Criticisms: open label; 43 patients crossed from culprit-only to multivessel; chronic occlusions were attempted in the multivessel arm, which may have added contrast, time and harm; and the primary follow-up was only thirty days.

291
00:25:38.340 --> 00:25:47.875
<v Tutor>What it changed: European guidance now advises against routine immediate revascularisation of non-culprit lesions in infarct-related cardiogenic shock.

292
00:25:47.875 --> 00:25:59.268
<v Tutor>Put that next to IABP-SHOCK II, which removed the routine balloon pump, and DanGer Shock, which found a survival benefit for the microaxial pump in a selected STEMI shock population.

293
00:25:59.268 --> 00:26:03.046
<v Tutor>Together they map how the evidence for shock has moved.

294
00:26:03.046 --> 00:26:13.540
<v Tutor>How to quote it: "In cardiogenic shock with multivessel disease, CULPRIT-SHOCK showed culprit-only PCI reduced thirty-day death or dialysis, 45.9 versus 55.4 percent."

295
00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:17.969
<v Examiner>This is from the 2021 second sitting, question nine.

296
00:26:17.969 --> 00:26:19.433
<v Examiner>I'll read it exactly.

297
00:26:19.433 --> 00:26:26.500
<v Examiner>"Outline your approach to the assessment and management of atrial fibrillation in the critically ill patient."
You have ten minutes.

298
00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:28.148
<v Candidate>Thank you.

299
00:26:28.148 --> 00:26:36.310
<v Candidate>I'll structure this as assessment, then management split into the unstable and the stable patient, then anticoagulation.

300
00:26:36.310 --> 00:26:37.521
<v Candidate>Assessment.

301
00:26:37.521 --> 00:26:46.257
<v Candidate>First, haemodynamic stability: looking for shock, ischaemia, heart failure or a fall in consciousness attributable to the rhythm.

302
00:26:46.257 --> 00:26:52.634
<v Candidate>Second, confirming the rhythm on a twelve-lead ECG and excluding flutter and pre-excitation.

303
00:26:52.634 --> 00:26:59.138
<v Candidate>Third, timing the onset from the monitor record, which matters for the cardioversion decision.

304
00:26:59.138 --> 00:27:14.250
<v Candidate>Fourth, looking for precipitants, which in the ICU are usually the acute illness: sepsis, hypovolaemia, catecholamine infusions, central lines in the right atrium, hypoxia, electrolyte loss and recent cardiac or thoracic surgery.

305
00:27:14.250 --> 00:27:25.983
<v Candidate>Investigations: potassium, magnesium, troponin, thyroid function, an arterial gas, a chest film, and a transthoracic echo for left ventricular function, valves and atrial size.

306
00:27:25.983 --> 00:27:27.832
<v Candidate>Management, unstable.

307
00:27:27.832 --> 00:27:37.333
<v Candidate>Synchronised DC cardioversion under sedation, starting at 120 to 150 joules biphasic, with amiodarone 300 milligrams if it fails or relapses.

308
00:27:37.333 --> 00:27:39.055
<v Candidate>Management, stable.

309
00:27:39.055 --> 00:27:45.240
<v Candidate>Treat the precipitant: replace potassium and magnesium, treat sepsis, and wean inotropes.

310
00:27:45.420 --> 00:27:54.326
<v Candidate>Then rate control to below 110, using a beta-blocker first line, or amiodarone if the blood pressure is marginal, and digoxin in poor LV function.

311
00:27:54.326 --> 00:27:58.663
<v Candidate>I'd avoid calcium channel blockers if the ejection fraction is low.

312
00:27:58.663 --> 00:28:04.620
<v Candidate>To guide fluid in the shocked patient, I'd use pulse pressure variation from the arterial line.

313
00:28:05.120 --> 00:28:05.977
<v Examiner>Stop there.

314
00:28:05.977 --> 00:28:08.998
<v Examiner>You said you'd use pulse pressure variation to guide fluid.

315
00:28:08.998 --> 00:28:10.080
<v Examiner>In this patient?

316
00:28:10.580 --> 00:28:11.205
<v Candidate>Ah.

317
00:28:11.205 --> 00:28:11.830
<v Candidate>No.

318
00:28:11.830 --> 00:28:14.785
<v Candidate>Pulse pressure variation needs sinus rhythm.

319
00:28:14.785 --> 00:28:21.662
<v Candidate>In atrial fibrillation the stroke volume varies from beat to beat with the filling time, so it's uninterpretable.

320
00:28:21.662 --> 00:28:30.300
<v Candidate>I'd use a passive leg raise with real-time cardiac output or velocity time integral on echo, or a small fluid challenge judged on stroke volume.

321
00:28:30.800 --> 00:28:31.486
<v Examiner>Better.

322
00:28:31.486 --> 00:28:34.183
<v Examiner>You promised anticoagulation and haven't delivered.

323
00:28:34.183 --> 00:28:36.880
<v Examiner>And what about cardioversion in the stable patient?

324
00:28:37.380 --> 00:28:38.726
<v Candidate>Anticoagulation.

325
00:28:38.726 --> 00:28:48.541
<v Candidate>I'd calculate the stroke risk with CHA2DS2-VA, and weigh it against the bleeding risk of the acute illness: recent surgery, thrombocytopenia, coagulopathy, procedures.

326
00:28:48.541 --> 00:28:53.869
<v Candidate>In the ICU, unfractionated heparin is usually the first choice because it's reversible.

327
00:28:53.869 --> 00:28:58.692
<v Candidate>For elective cardioversion, the onset must be clearly under twenty-four hours.

328
00:28:58.692 --> 00:29:08.956
<v Candidate>Otherwise the patient needs three weeks of anticoagulation or a TOE excluding left atrial appendage thrombus, and anticoagulation continues for at least four weeks afterwards.

329
00:29:08.956 --> 00:29:19.332
<v Candidate>And I'd add that in the critically ill, cardioversion often fails or relapses while the driver persists, so rate control and treating the cause is usually the better first step.

330
00:29:19.332 --> 00:29:24.940
<v Candidate>Much of this AF reverts as the patient recovers, and it should be reviewed before discharge.

331
00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:26.877
<v Examiner>Here's the marking.

332
00:29:26.877 --> 00:29:35.660
<v Examiner>Assessment carries roughly three marks: stability, the ECG with exclusion of pre-excitation, onset time, precipitants and targeted tests, each with a reason.

333
00:29:35.660 --> 00:29:37.204
<v Examiner>You got most of that.

334
00:29:37.204 --> 00:29:41.568
<v Examiner>Your test list needed the reasons attached, as the 2026 question demanded.

335
00:29:41.568 --> 00:29:44.283
<v Examiner>Unstable management carries one and a half.

336
00:29:44.283 --> 00:29:45.294
<v Examiner>You got it.

337
00:29:45.294 --> 00:29:47.743
<v Examiner>Stable management carries about three.

338
00:29:47.743 --> 00:29:53.119
<v Examiner>The precipitant first, rate control with named drugs and their limits, and the relapse point.

339
00:29:53.119 --> 00:29:59.985
<v Examiner>You got there, but the pulse pressure variation slip would have cost you a credibility half mark before you recovered it.

340
00:29:59.985 --> 00:30:02.806
<v Examiner>In a written paper, you don't get to recover.

341
00:30:02.806 --> 00:30:05.042
<v Examiner>Anticoagulation carries about two.

342
00:30:05.042 --> 00:30:11.962
<v Examiner>The score, the bleeding balance in the critically ill, the agent, the twenty-four hour rule and the four weeks afterwards.

343
00:30:11.962 --> 00:30:14.517
<v Examiner>Your version came late but was complete.

344
00:30:14.517 --> 00:30:23.566
<v Examiner>The last half mark goes to the one sentence that shows you know the ICU is different: most new atrial fibrillation in the critically ill is driven by the illness.

345
00:30:23.566 --> 00:30:26.866
<v Examiner>Verdict: as written, around six and a half out of ten.

346
00:30:26.866 --> 00:30:33.200
<v Examiner>With anticoagulation planned up front and the pulse pressure variation line removed, seven and a half to eight.

347
00:30:33.380 --> 00:30:42.740
<v Examiner>The one fix: write a four-part skeleton in the first minute, assessment, unstable, stable, anticoagulation, so no part gets left until time runs out.

348
00:30:44.140 --> 00:30:50.127
<v Tutor>Now the syllabus round-up: six items from this week's block that the episodes haven't touched yet.

349
00:30:50.127 --> 00:30:53.177
<v Tutor>First, hypertensive crisis with a renal focus.

350
00:30:53.177 --> 00:30:55.492
<v Tutor>It appears in six past questions.

351
00:30:55.492 --> 00:31:01.648
<v Tutor>A hypertensive emergency is severe hypertension, usually above 180 over 120, with acute organ damage.

352
00:31:01.648 --> 00:31:06.449
<v Tutor>Severe hypertension without organ damage is an urgency and is treated orally.

353
00:31:06.449 --> 00:31:08.821
<v Tutor>The renal faces to name are these.

354
00:31:08.821 --> 00:31:15.034
<v Tutor>Acute kidney injury with thrombotic microangiopathy: fragments on the film, low platelets, a high LDH.

355
00:31:15.034 --> 00:31:16.897
<v Tutor>Scleroderma renal crisis.

356
00:31:16.897 --> 00:31:20.625
<v Tutor>Renovascular disease presenting as flash pulmonary oedema.

357
00:31:20.625 --> 00:31:22.093
<v Tutor>And pre-eclampsia.

358
00:31:22.093 --> 00:31:25.087
<v Tutor>Scleroderma renal crisis is the classic trap.

359
00:31:25.087 --> 00:31:31.299
<v Tutor>The treatment is an ACE inhibitor, classically captopril, titrated up even while the creatinine rises.

360
00:31:31.299 --> 00:31:34.632
<v Tutor>Stopping it because the creatinine climbs is wrong.

361
00:31:34.632 --> 00:31:41.183
<v Tutor>The general target: reduce mean arterial pressure by no more than about 20 to 25 percent in the first hours.

362
00:31:41.183 --> 00:31:52.140
<v Tutor>The two exceptions are aortic dissection, where you go lower and faster with heart rate control first, and acute ischaemic stroke, where you go more cautiously depending on thrombolysis.

363
00:31:52.320 --> 00:32:04.660
<v Tutor>Agents to name, all titratable intravenously: labetalol, clevidipine or nicardipine, glyceryl trinitrate for pulmonary oedema, and sodium nitroprusside, with its cyanide and thiocyanate toxicity.

364
00:32:04.660 --> 00:32:09.280
<v Tutor>Oh's chapter twenty-seven covers vasodilators and antihypertensives.

365
00:32:09.680 --> 00:32:15.214
<v Tutor>Second, infective endocarditis: four past questions, the latest in the 2026 second sitting.

366
00:32:15.214 --> 00:32:20.692
<v Tutor>The governing guideline is the 2023 European Society of Cardiology endocarditis guideline.

367
00:32:20.692 --> 00:32:24.828
<v Tutor>The intensivist's questions are when to operate, and how to image.

368
00:32:24.828 --> 00:32:34.163
<v Tutor>Emergency surgery, within twenty-four hours, for aortic or mitral endocarditis with valve dysfunction causing refractory pulmonary oedema or cardiogenic shock.

369
00:32:34.163 --> 00:32:38.020
<v Tutor>Urgent surgery, within three to five days, for heart failure.

370
00:32:38.020 --> 00:32:44.896
<v Tutor>Also for locally uncontrolled infection: abscess, false aneurysm, fistula, an enlarging vegetation or new AV block.

371
00:32:44.896 --> 00:32:49.256
<v Tutor>And for a vegetation of 10 millimetres or more after an embolic event.

372
00:32:49.256 --> 00:32:55.013
<v Tutor>For right-sided disease, residual tricuspid vegetations above 20 millimetres are an indication.

373
00:32:55.013 --> 00:33:07.814
<v Tutor>Imaging: transthoracic echo first, transoesophageal echo for prosthetic valves or a negative transthoracic study, FDG PET-CT for possible prosthetic valve endocarditis, and brain imaging when there are neurological signs.

374
00:33:07.814 --> 00:33:11.000
<v Tutor>Device infection means removing the whole system.

375
00:33:11.000 --> 00:33:15.360
<v Tutor>Prosthetic valve endocarditis needs at least six weeks of antibiotics.

376
00:33:15.540 --> 00:33:24.443
<v Tutor>In selected stable patients, the guideline allows a switch to oral therapy after two weeks of intravenous treatment and a transoesophageal echo showing no progression.

377
00:33:24.443 --> 00:33:26.580
<v Tutor>In Oh's, it's chapter thirty-four.

378
00:33:26.980 --> 00:33:34.179
<v Tutor>Third and fourth together: pericarditis, which has five past questions, and pericardiocentesis, which has four.

379
00:33:34.179 --> 00:33:40.834
<v Tutor>The new document is the 2025 European Society of Cardiology guideline on myocarditis and pericarditis.

380
00:33:40.834 --> 00:33:50.634
<v Tutor>Acute pericarditis needs two of four: pleuritic chest pain, a friction rub, new widespread ST elevation or PR depression, and a new or worsening effusion.

381
00:33:50.634 --> 00:33:57.772
<v Tutor>Treatment is a non-steroidal or aspirin with colchicine, colchicine for around three months to cut recurrence.

382
00:33:57.772 --> 00:33:59.769
<v Tutor>Steroids are second line.

383
00:33:59.769 --> 00:34:02.915
<v Tutor>Tamponade is clinical and echocardiographic.

384
00:34:02.915 --> 00:34:09.085
<v Tutor>Hypotension, raised venous pressure, pulsus paradoxus of more than ten millimetres of mercury.

385
00:34:09.085 --> 00:34:20.700
<v Tutor>On echo: right atrial collapse in systole, right ventricular collapse in diastole, a plethoric inferior vena cava, and exaggerated respiratory variation in mitral and tricuspid inflow.

386
00:34:20.700 --> 00:34:27.052
<v Tutor>Pericardiocentesis is echo-guided, usually subxiphoid or apical, with a pigtail left in to drain.

387
00:34:27.052 --> 00:34:29.412
<v Tutor>The traps the examiners reward.

388
00:34:29.412 --> 00:34:35.219
<v Tutor>One: after cardiac surgery, tamponade is often a localised clot compressing one chamber.

389
00:34:35.219 --> 00:34:40.180
<v Tutor>Echo can miss it, and the answer is surgical re-exploration, not a needle.

390
00:34:40.360 --> 00:34:47.922
<v Tutor>Two: in type A dissection with tamponade, a needle drain can precipitate rupture, so the answer is the operating theatre.

391
00:34:47.922 --> 00:34:52.729
<v Tutor>Three: intubation and positive pressure can cause cardiovascular collapse.

392
00:34:52.729 --> 00:34:58.240
<v Tutor>Keep the patient breathing spontaneously, load with fluid, and drain first if you can.

393
00:34:58.640 --> 00:35:02.858
<v Tutor>Fifth, pulmonary hypertension and the failing right ventricle.

394
00:35:02.858 --> 00:35:08.402
<v Tutor>Three past questions, plus the perioperative right heart questions of 2017 and 2026.

395
00:35:08.402 --> 00:35:17.442
<v Tutor>The 2022 European respiratory and cardiology guideline lowered the definition: mean pulmonary artery pressure above 20 millimetres of mercury.

396
00:35:17.442 --> 00:35:24.251
<v Tutor>Pre-capillary disease adds a pulmonary vascular resistance above 2 Wood units with a wedge of 15 or less.

397
00:35:24.251 --> 00:35:27.927
<v Tutor>In the unit, the right ventricle fails from pressure.

398
00:35:27.927 --> 00:35:31.905
<v Tutor>The management principles form a structure examiners like.

399
00:35:31.905 --> 00:35:39.558
<v Tutor>First, remove what raises pulmonary vascular resistance: hypoxia, hypercapnia, acidosis, high PEEP and over-distension.

400
00:35:39.558 --> 00:35:47.874
<v Tutor>Second, protect right coronary perfusion by keeping systemic pressure above pulmonary pressure, with noradrenaline or vasopressin.

401
00:35:47.874 --> 00:35:49.803
<v Tutor>Third, optimise preload.

402
00:35:49.803 --> 00:35:56.853
<v Tutor>The failing right ventricle is usually over-full, so diuresis or ultrafiltration often helps more than fluid.

403
00:35:56.853 --> 00:36:01.735
<v Tutor>Fourth, support contractility with dobutamine, milrinone or levosimendan.

404
00:36:01.735 --> 00:36:07.761
<v Tutor>Fifth, give a selective pulmonary vasodilator: inhaled nitric oxide or inhaled epoprostenol.

405
00:36:07.761 --> 00:36:12.160
<v Tutor>Sixth, think early about mechanical support, VA ECMO as a bridge.

406
00:36:12.340 --> 00:36:19.715
<v Tutor>And name the danger: induction and intubation in severe pulmonary hypertension is one of the highest-risk moments in intensive care.

407
00:36:19.715 --> 00:36:21.980
<v Tutor>In Oh's, it's chapter thirty-three.

408
00:36:22.380 --> 00:36:23.618
<v Tutor>Pause and answer.

409
00:36:23.618 --> 00:36:29.514
<v Tutor>A patient with known pulmonary arterial hypertension is hypoxic and hypotensive with a dilated right ventricle.

410
00:36:29.514 --> 00:36:32.932
<v Tutor>Name three things that will make them worse in the next hour.

411
00:36:32.932 --> 00:36:34.220
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

412
00:36:39.620 --> 00:37:00.900
<v Tutor>Any three of: a large fluid bolus, which dilates the right ventricle further and shifts the septum; induction and positive pressure ventilation, with the fall in systemic pressure and rise in right ventricular afterload; hypoxia, hypercapnia or acidosis; high PEEP; and systemic vasodilators that drop the pressure perfusing the right coronary artery.

413
00:37:01.300 --> 00:37:04.541
<v Tutor>Sixth, beyond the cardiac core, vascular surgery.

414
00:37:04.541 --> 00:37:06.076
<v Tutor>Two past questions.

415
00:37:06.076 --> 00:37:10.114
<v Tutor>Oh's chapter thirty-five covers it with acute aortic syndromes.

416
00:37:10.114 --> 00:37:20.690
<v Tutor>For ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm, the principles are permissive hypotension in the conscious patient, with systolic pressure around 70 to 90, until the aorta is controlled.

417
00:37:20.690 --> 00:37:22.964
<v Tutor>Avoid large crystalloid volumes.

418
00:37:22.964 --> 00:37:28.651
<v Tutor>Endovascular repair is preferred where anatomy allows; the IMPROVE trial is the one to name.

419
00:37:28.651 --> 00:37:31.152
<v Tutor>After repair, watch for four things.

420
00:37:31.152 --> 00:37:36.896
<v Tutor>Abdominal compartment syndrome: intra-abdominal pressure above 20 with new organ dysfunction.

421
00:37:36.896 --> 00:37:40.421
<v Tutor>Colonic ischaemia: bloody diarrhoea, a rising lactate.

422
00:37:40.421 --> 00:37:42.013
<v Tutor>Acute kidney injury.

423
00:37:42.013 --> 00:37:46.448
<v Tutor>And, after thoracic or thoracoabdominal repair, spinal cord ischaemia.

424
00:37:46.448 --> 00:37:49.405
<v Tutor>That last one is the high-yield ICU problem.

425
00:37:49.405 --> 00:37:54.409
<v Tutor>Spinal cord perfusion pressure equals mean arterial pressure minus CSF pressure.

426
00:37:54.409 --> 00:38:05.838
<v Tutor>So you drain CSF to a pressure around 10 millimetres of mercury, limiting the drainage rate to avoid subdural haemorrhage, and you raise the mean arterial pressure, often to 80 to 90 or higher.

427
00:38:05.838 --> 00:38:14.140
<v Tutor>If the legs weaken after the operation, that is an emergency: raise the pressure, open the drain, and correct haemoglobin and oxygenation.

428
00:38:14.540 --> 00:38:21.031
<v Tutor>Before Wednesday: today's set includes the 2026 first-sitting question with only an ECG and an eighty-two-year-old.

429
00:38:21.031 --> 00:38:24.725
<v Tutor>Write it in ten minutes using the structure from this episode.

430
00:38:24.725 --> 00:38:27.258
<v Tutor>Assessment with a reason for every test.

431
00:38:27.258 --> 00:38:30.425
<v Tutor>Then the unstable patient, split into fast and slow.

432
00:38:30.425 --> 00:38:33.169
<v Tutor>Then the specific treatments for the causes.

433
00:38:33.169 --> 00:38:39.290
<v Tutor>And do the daily ECG drill out loud, naming rate, rhythm, axis, intervals and one diagnosis before you look.

434
00:38:39.290 --> 00:38:40.768
<v Tutor>That's it for today.

435
00:38:40.768 --> 00:38:42.140
<v Tutor>See you next time.
